# §0. Statement

**Theorem (endpoint divided-difference regularity).** Fix the chart D⁺ of
GP-DER-045/046 and suppose the closed segment [M, S] × {0} ⊂ rD⁺ (true for the
certified chart, which contains the pair axis). Define, under Q_{6,r},

A_M = f_xx(M)/r, B_M = f_xy(M)/r, C_M = f_yy(M), and likewise at S.

Then, **Q_{6,r}-almost surely, as exact pathwise identities:**

\[
\begin{aligned}
A_M &= -1 + \tfrac{r}{12}c_{40} + R^A_M, &\quad
A_S &= +1 + \tfrac{r}{12}c_{40} + R^A_S,\\
B_M &= -\tfrac{a}{2} + \tfrac{r}{12}c_{31} + R^B_M, &\quad
B_S &= +\tfrac{a}{2} + \tfrac{r}{12}c_{31} + R^B_S,\\
C_M &= q - \tfrac{r}{2}w + \tfrac{r^2}{8}c_{22} + R^C_M, &\quad
C_S &= q + \tfrac{r}{2}w + \tfrac{r^2}{8}c_{22} + R^C_S,
\end{aligned}
\]

with every remainder bounded **pathwise** by

\[
|R^\bullet_\bullet|\;\le\;\tfrac14\,r^2\,H_{5,r}(D^+).
\]

Consequently: (i) the joint family \((A_M,B_M,C_M,A_S,B_S,C_S,J_r)\) has
**uniformly bounded moments of every fixed order** for r ≤ min(r_GR, r₅);
(ii) it **converges in distribution** as r → 0 to
\((-1,\,-a_\infty/2,\,Q_L,\,+1,\,+a_\infty/2,\,Q_L,\,J_\infty)\) with
\(J_\infty \sim \gamma_0\); (iii) \(D_M = A_MC_M - rB_M^2 \to -Q_L\) and
\(D_S \to +Q_L\) in distribution, and **every fixed polynomial in the six
endpoint variables is uniformly integrable** — in particular
\(W_r^{exact}/r^2\).

This is precisely the package GP-DER-118-v1.3 §5 requires and CL-AUD-203
found absent: limits, signs, uniform moments, the joint object with J_r, and
the UI input to §6.

# §1. The conditional cancellation, performed

The heart of CL-AUD-203's finding (ii): unconditionally,
\(\mathrm{Var}(f_{xx}(M)/r)\) diverges. The boundedness is a **consequence of
the gradient pins**, and it is pathwise, not distributional.

The field is a.s. C^∞; on the pin event, \(f_x(\pm r/2, 0) = 0\) exactly.
Taylor \(f_x(\cdot,0)\) at the midpoint to third order with integral remainder
(remainders involve only \(\partial^{(5,0)}f\) on the segment, hence are
dominated by \(H_{5,r}\)):

**Odd difference** (both terms zero):

\[
0 = f_x(\tfrac r2)-f_x(-\tfrac r2)
= r\,f_{xx}(0) + \tfrac{r^3}{24}c_{40} + E_1,
\qquad |E_1|\le 2\cdot\frac{(r/2)^4}{24}H_5=\frac{r^4H_5}{192},
\]

giving the cancellation identity

\[
\boxed{\;f_{xx}(0) = -\tfrac{r^2}{24}c_{40} - \tfrac{E_1}{r},\qquad
\Bigl|\tfrac{E_1}{r}\Bigr|\le \tfrac{r^3H_5}{192}.\;}
\]

The midpoint second derivative is **conditionally O(r²)** — the projection
cancellation CL-AUD-203 §2(ii) asked for, exhibited as one displayed line.

**Even sum** (both terms zero):
\(0 = 2f_x(0) + \tfrac{r^2}{4}f_{xxx}(0) + E_4\), \(|E_4|\le r^4H_5/192\),
so \(f_x(0) = -\tfrac{r^2}{8}f_{xxx}(0) - E_4/2\).

**Value pins** (difference \(-r^3/6\)):
\(-\tfrac{r^3}{6} = r f_x(0) + \tfrac{r^3}{24}f_{xxx}(0) + E_5\),
\(|E_5|\le 2\cdot\frac{(r/2)^5}{120}H_5 = \frac{r^5H_5}{1920}\). Substituting,

\[
-\tfrac{r^3}{12}f_{xxx}(0) = -\tfrac{r^3}{6} + \tfrac{r}{2}E_4 - E_5
\;\Longrightarrow\;
f_{xxx}(0) = 2 + e_3,\qquad
|e_3| \le 12\Bigl(\tfrac{1}{384}+\tfrac{1}{1920}\Bigr)r^2H_5 = \tfrac{9}{240}r^2H_5.
\]

The prescribed third divided difference tends to 2 with an explicit rate —
note it uses **only the difference** of the value pins, so nothing here
depends on b.

# §2. The six identities

**A_M.** Taylor \(f_{xx}(\cdot,0)\) at 0 to second order, remainder in
\(\partial^{(5,0)}\):

\[
f_{xx}(M)=f_{xx}(0)-\tfrac r2 f_{xxx}(0)+\tfrac{r^2}{8}c_{40}+E_2,
\qquad |E_2|\le\frac{(r/2)^3}{6}H_5=\frac{r^3H_5}{48}.
\]

Insert §1's boxes:

\[
f_{xx}(M) = -r + r^2c_{40}\bigl(\tfrac18-\tfrac1{24}\bigr)
-\tfrac r2 e_3 - \tfrac{E_1}{r} + E_2
= -r + \tfrac{r^2}{12}c_{40} + rR^A_M,
\]

\[
|R^A_M| \le \Bigl(\tfrac{1}{192} + \tfrac{1}{48} + \tfrac{9}{480}\Bigr)r^2H_5
< \tfrac14 r^2 H_5. \qquad
\textbf{A_M} = -1+\tfrac{r}{12}c_{40}+R^A_M.
\]

At S the odd-order terms flip sign: \(A_S = +1+\tfrac{r}{12}c_{40}+R^A_S\),
same bound. **Signs: A_M(0) = −1, A_S(0) = +1.** ✓

**B_M.** The y-gradient pins \(f_y(\pm r/2,0)=0\): odd difference gives
\(f_{xy}(0) = -\tfrac{r^2}{24}c_{31} - E_7/r\), \(|E_7|\le r^4H_5/192\)
(remainder in \(\partial^{(4,1)}\!f\), order five ✓); even sum gives
\(f_y(0) = -\tfrac{r^2}{8}a + O(r^4H_5)\) (recorded for completeness; not
needed below). Taylor \(f_{xy}\) to second order
(\(\partial_x^2 f_{xy} = c_{31}\), remainder in \(\partial^{(4,1)}\)):

\[
f_{xy}(M)=f_{xy}(0)-\tfrac r2\,a+\tfrac{r^2}{8}c_{31}+E_8,\quad
|E_8|\le\tfrac{r^3H_5}{48}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\textbf{B_M}=-\tfrac a2+\tfrac{r}{12}c_{31}+R^B_M,
\]

\(|R^B_M|\le\bigl(\tfrac1{192}+\tfrac1{48}\bigr)r^2H_5 = \tfrac{5}{192}r^2H_5\),
and \(B_S = +\tfrac a2+\tfrac{r}{12}c_{31}+R^B_S\). **B_S(0) = −B_M(0) = a/2**,
the antisymmetric degeneracy of the limit law, now explicit rather than
troubling. ✓

**C_M.** One Taylor step only (\(\partial_x f_{yy} = w\),
\(\partial_x^2f_{yy}=c_{22}\), remainder \(\partial^{(3,2)}\), order five):

\[
\textbf{C_M}=q-\tfrac r2 w+\tfrac{r^2}{8}c_{22}+R^C_M,\qquad
|R^C_M|\le\tfrac{r^3H_5}{48},
\]

and \(C_S = q+\tfrac r2 w+\tfrac{r^2}{8}c_{22}+R^C_S\).
**C_M(0) = C_S(0) = q → Q_L.** ✓

All five limiting values and signs claimed by the frozen §5 are recovered,
each with an explicit O(r) first correction that **coincides exactly with the
degree-four model's endpoint Hessian entries** (−1 + rc₄₀/12, −a/2 + rc₃₁/12,
q − rw/2 + r²c₂₂/8) — the exact field and the Q4 model agree at the endpoint-
Hessian level to O(r²H₅), which is stronger than §5 needed and is
independently comforting for §7's determinant-transfer step.

# §3. Uniform moments, the joint family, and UI — the §6 inputs

Write the six-vector as \(V_r = \Phi(J_r) + r\Psi(J_r) + R_r\): Φ affine and
**r-independent** (entries −1, +1, ∓a/2, q), Ψ linear with fixed coefficients
(c₄₀/12, c₃₁/12, ∓w/2 + rc₂₂/8-part), and \(|R_r|\le\tfrac14 r^2H_{5,r}\)
pathwise.

**Moments.** \(\|V_r\|_{L^p} \le \|\Phi(J_r)\|_{L^p} + r\|\Psi(J_r)\|_{L^p}
+ \tfrac14 r^2\|H_{5,r}\|_{L^p}\). The first two are uniform by GP-DER-044
§2's uniform nine-jet moments (the PROVEN scope). For the third: with the
repaired GT5 tail,

\[
E[H_{5,r}^p] = \int_0^\infty p t^{p-1}Q_{6,r}(H_{5,r}>t)\,dt
\le t_0^p + \int_{t_0}^\infty p t^{p-1}C_5e^{-c_5t^2}dt
=: M_p < \infty,
\]

**uniformly in r ≤ r₅** — a uniform Gaussian tail beyond a uniform threshold
has uniform moments of every order. Hence uniform moments of every fixed order
for \((V_r, J_r)\) jointly — and the joint object \(J_r\)-plus-endpoints,
which CL-AUD-203 §2(iv) noted was never even defined in the cited source, is
here **constructed**: \((V_r, J_r) = (\Phi \oplus \mathrm{id})(J_r) +
(r\Psi(J_r)+R_r,\,0)\).

**Convergence in distribution.** \(J_r \Rightarrow J_\infty \sim \gamma_0\)
(044 §2); Φ⊕id is continuous and fixed, so
\((\Phi\oplus\mathrm{id})(J_r) \Rightarrow (\Phi\oplus\mathrm{id})(J_\infty)\)
by continuous mapping; the perturbation
\(\|r\Psi(J_r)+R_r\|_{L^2} \le rK_1 + \tfrac14 r^2\sqrt{M_2} \to 0\), and an
\(L^2\)-vanishing perturbation preserves the weak limit. The limit law is
exactly the partially degenerate law the frozen §5 describes — deterministic
±1 axial entries, antisymmetric B, common C = Q_L — with the degeneracy now a
computed image, needing no PD/Schur transfer (CL's finding (iii) dissolved
rather than repaired).

**Determinants and UI.** \(D_M = A_MC_M - rB_M^2\) and \(D_S\) are fixed
polynomials in \((V_r, r)\); by the above, \(D_M \Rightarrow (-1)\cdot Q_L =
-Q_L\) and \(D_S \Rightarrow +Q_L\). Any fixed polynomial in \(V_r\) is
bounded in every \(L^p\) uniformly in r; \(L^{1+\epsilon}\)-boundedness gives
**uniform integrability**, in particular for
\(W_r^{exact}/r^2 = |D_MD_S|\mathbf 1_{type}\le|D_MD_S|\). Together with the
type-indicator convergence already verified in-doc at the nondegenerate
\(Q_L = 0\) null boundary, this supplies every input §6 consumes.

